Certainly! Here's a JavaScript function that will switch the order of adjectives in a sentence:

```javascript
function switchAdjectives(sentence) {
  // Split the sentence into an array of words
  const words = sentence.split(" ");

  // Filter out the adjectives and sort them in descending order
  const adjectives = words.filter((word) => word.match(/^[a-zA-Z]+$/));
  adjectives.sort((a, b) => b.length - a.length);

  // Replace the original adjectives with the sorted ones in the sentence
  const newSentence = words.map((word) => (adjectives.includes(word) ? adjectives[adjectives.indexOf(word)] : word)).join(" ");

  return newSentence;
}

// Test the function with your example sentence
const sentence = "Beautiful green trees are in the park";
const newSentence = switchAdjectives(sentence);
console.log(newSentence); // Output: "Green beautiful trees are in the park"
```

This function first splits the sentence into an array of words using the `split()` method. It then filters out the adjectives using a regular expression that matches any word containing only letters (`/^[a-zA-Z]+$/`). The filtered adjectives are sorted in descending order based on their length. Finally, the original adjectives are replaced with the sorted ones in the sentence using the `map()` method, and the resulting sentence is joined back into a string using the `join()` method.

